W: 1. As I went down to Derby upon a market day,
W: I spied the biggest ram, sir, that ever was fed on hay.
W:
W: Chorus:
W: Hey ringle dingle, hey ringle day,
W: It was the biggest ram, sir, that ever was fed on hay.
W:
W: 2. The horns upon this ram, sir, they reached up to the moon.
W: A lad went up in April, and didn't get down till June. (Chorus)
W:
W: 3. The fleece upon this ram, sir. it reached up to the sky.
W: The eagles made their nests there, you could hear the young ones cry. (Chorus)
W:
W: 4. And all the boys of Derby come beggin' a-for his eyes.
W: To kick around the street. sir, cause they was football size. (Chorus)
W:
W: 5. And all the women of Derby come beggin' a-for his ears,
W: To make them leather aprons, to last for forty years. (Chorus)
W:
W: 6. And all the men of Derby come beggin‘ a-for his tail,
W: To ring St. George's pass'in bell, from top of Derby gaol. (Chorus)
W:
W: 7. lt took all the boys of Derby to carry away his bones.
W: Took all the maids of Derby to roll away his stones. (Chorus)
W:
W: 8. Now the butcher that killed this ram, sir, was up to his thighs in blood.
W: The boy that held the basin was washed away in the ﬂood. (Chorus)
W:
W: 9. And now me song is over, I got no more to say.
W: just give us eggs and brandy, and we'll be on our way. (Chorus)
W: (Repeat chorus)
